<%# The one place in this app where something genuinely changes underneath a page that is
    already open, and the thing that lets the page notice. #249.

    Fifteen MCP tools carry `scope :write` and four of them write to a workout you may be
    looking at right now -- create_set, complete_set, update_set, delete_set -- plus
    create_workout and generate_program_week, which can write the session itself. So: the
    phone is propped against your bag, an assistant logs the set you just told it about,
    and the screen goes on showing what was true when it loaded. Nothing crashes, because
    every route is account-scoped and every write goes through own_set; the screen is
    simply behind, in a way that reads as the app being wrong rather than as the app being
    late. The worst of the three is a corrected weight, because it is silently wrong rather
    than visibly broken: the plate math under it is for a load that is no longer prescribed.

    Polling rather than a channel, which is the answer #248 settled: websockets need Falcon
    under the web server, and buying full duplex for a problem where the browser has nothing
    to push is paying at the wrong end. The staleness window that matters here is "since I
    last looked at the screen", not "since the last frame", and fifteen seconds closes it.

    What makes this cheap enough to be obviously right is the fingerprint. The request
    carries the digest of the sets as this page rendered them; unchanged, the route answers
    204 and htmx does nothing at all, so the common case costs one small request and touches
    no DOM. There is no re-render on a timer, no open <details> snapping shut mid-set, and
    no swap landing between a thumb and a Done button.

    hx-target is #lift-panels and the swap is innerHTML, which is load-bearing rather than
    incidental: it replaces the panels and leaves the scroller itself alive, so scrollLeft
    survives. Swapping #session-body instead would destroy the scroller and drop a lifter
    back on lift one -- every fifteen seconds, which is the bug #235 removed, reintroduced
    on a timer. The progress header and this element come back out of band beside them.

    This element replaces itself because it carries state: the `since` it was rendered with
    is stale the moment a swap lands, and a poller still asking about the old digest would
    report the same news forever. %>
<div id="session-poll"<%== ' hx-swap-oob="true"' if defined?(oob) && oob %>
     hx-get="/workouts/<%= workout_id %>/session/changes?since=<%= fingerprint %>"
     hx-trigger="every 15s"
     hx-target="#lift-panels"
     hx-swap="innerHTML"></div>
